1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,712
<font color="#FFFFFF">Offline reinforcement learning in domains like
healthcare and education must estimate</font>

2
00:00:05,792 --> 00:00:09,448
<font color="#FFFFFF">the environment's transition dynamics from fixed
batch data,</font>

3
00:00:09,528 --> 00:00:14,989
<font color="#FFFFFF">yet standard maximum likelihood estimates give high
variance policies that make dangerous</font>

4
00:00:15,069 --> 00:00:16,856
<font color="#FFFFFF">mistakes where data is sparse.</font>

5
00:00:16,936 --> 00:00:21,588
<font color="#FFFFFF">This paper introduces Bayesian Inverse Transition
Learning, a gradient free,</font>

6
00:00:21,668 --> 00:00:27,316
<font color="#FFFFFF">constraint based method that uses an expert's near
optimal demonstrations to clip a Bayesian</font>

7
00:00:27,396 --> 00:00:29,744
<font color="#FFFFFF">posterior over the transition dynamics.</font>

8
00:00:29,824 --> 00:00:33,604
<font color="#FFFFFF">The result is a distribution of dynamics that
guarantees safe,</font>

9
00:00:33,684 --> 00:00:35,161
<font color="#FFFFFF">high performing policies,</font>

10
00:00:35,241 --> 00:00:40,888
<font color="#FFFFFF">recovering one hundred percent accuracy on states
where the best action is known and cutting</font>

11
00:00:40,968 --> 00:00:43,690
<font color="#FFFFFF">policy variance dramatically across datasets.</font>

12
00:00:44,070 --> 00:00:48,167
<font color="#FFFFFF">In fields like healthcare and education we cannot
experiment freely,</font>

13
00:00:48,247 --> 00:00:53,633
<font color="#FFFFFF">so offline reinforcement learning must learn the
environment's transition dynamics purely</font>

14
00:00:53,713 --> 00:00:56,274
<font color="#FFFFFF">from a fixed batch of collected experience.</font>

15
00:00:56,354 --> 00:01:01,618
<font color="#FFFFFF">The trouble is that this data only covers the
actions the original users actually took,</font>

16
00:01:01,698 --> 00:01:04,689
<font color="#FFFFFF">leaving much of the state and action space unseen.</font>

17
00:01:04,769 --> 00:01:09,909
<font color="#FFFFFF">Standard maximum likelihood estimates of the
dynamics therefore produce policies that</font>

18
00:01:09,989 --> 00:01:15,192
<font color="#FFFFFF">swing wildly from dataset to dataset and can
recommend genuinely unsafe actions in the</font>

19
00:01:15,272 --> 00:01:16,850
<font color="#FFFFFF">regions where data is thin.</font>

20
00:01:17,230 --> 00:01:21,169
<font color="#FFFFFF">The key insight is that the people who generate
this offline data,</font>

21
00:01:21,249 --> 00:01:24,458
<font color="#FFFFFF">such as clinicians, are usually acting near optimally.</font>

22
00:01:24,538 --> 00:01:28,721
<font color="#FFFFFF">Their choices quietly encode which actions are good
and which are bad,</font>

23
00:01:28,801 --> 00:01:33,592
<font color="#FFFFFF">yet a plain maximum likelihood fit of the dynamics
throws that information away.</font>

24
00:01:33,672 --> 00:01:40,596
<font color="#FFFFFF">Earlier gradient based inverse reinforcement
learning methods try to recover an expert's belief about the dynamics,</font>

25
00:01:40,676 --> 00:01:45,894
<font color="#FFFFFF">but they never connect their estimate back to the
true environment and they suffer from</font>

26
00:01:45,974 --> 00:01:48,269
<font color="#FFFFFF">the fragility of gradient optimization.</font>

27
00:01:48,349 --> 00:01:52,410
<font color="#FFFFFF">We want to exploit the expert signal directly and
without gradients.</font>

28
00:01:52,790 --> 00:01:55,754
<font color="#FFFFFF">This work introduces Inverse Transition Learning,</font>

29
00:01:55,834 --> 00:01:58,611
<font color="#FFFFFF">a gradient free and constraint based approach.</font>

30
00:01:58,691 --> 00:02:04,450
<font color="#FFFFFF">It converts a near optimal expert policy into a set
of constraints on the transition dynamics,</font>

31
00:02:04,530 --> 00:02:09,916
<font color="#FFFFFF">and then clips a Bayesian posterior over the
dynamics so that every sampled model yields</font>

32
00:02:09,996 --> 00:02:12,028
<font color="#FFFFFF">a safe and high performing policy.</font>

33
00:02:12,108 --> 00:02:13,581
<font color="#FFFFFF">Beyond the method itself,</font>

34
00:02:13,661 --> 00:02:18,985
<font color="#FFFFFF">the paper carefully analyzes when and why maximum
likelihood estimation of the dynamics</font>

35
00:02:19,065 --> 00:02:21,407
<font color="#FFFFFF">breaks down under uneven data coverage,</font>

36
00:02:21,487 --> 00:02:27,060
<font color="#FFFFFF">and it shows how combining the constraints with
uncertainty produces an informative ranking</font>

37
00:02:27,140 --> 00:02:30,290
<font color="#FFFFFF">of actions in the states where the expert is unsure.</font>

38
00:02:30,670 --> 00:02:35,142
<font color="#FFFFFF">Given the rewards, an epsilon optimal expert
policy, and the batch data,</font>

39
00:02:35,222 --> 00:02:40,579
<font color="#FFFFFF">we use the closed form Bellman equations to write
constraints that demand the value of</font>

40
00:02:40,659 --> 00:02:45,321
<font color="#FFFFFF">actions the expert takes exceed the value of
actions the expert never took.</font>

41
00:02:45,401 --> 00:02:51,643
<font color="#FFFFFF">Any dynamics satisfying these constraints recovers
what we call the expert's epsilon ball structure.</font>

42
00:02:51,723 --> 00:02:57,523
<font color="#FFFFFF">We then place a Dirichlet multinomial posterior
over the dynamics and use rejection sampling,</font>

43
00:02:57,603 --> 00:03:00,937
<font color="#FFFFFF">keeping only the samples that satisfy the constraints.</font>

44
00:03:01,017 --> 00:03:06,248
<font color="#FFFFFF">The surviving samples form a clipped posterior that
respects the expert's knowledge.</font>

45
00:03:06,328 --> 00:03:11,622
<font color="#FFFFFF">Separate constraint sets handle a fully optimal
expert and a partially uncertain one,</font>

46
00:03:11,702 --> 00:03:18,640
<font color="#FFFFFF">with a tunable slack term that enforces the
structure even where action non linearity would otherwise break it.</font>

47
00:03:19,020 --> 00:03:24,599
<font color="#FFFFFF">All experiments run on a synthetic tabular Markov
decision process with fifteen states</font>

48
00:03:24,679 --> 00:03:27,099
<font color="#FFFFFF">plus a terminal state and six actions,</font>

49
00:03:27,179 --> 00:03:30,258
<font color="#FFFFFF">using a discount factor of zero point nine five.</font>

50
00:03:30,338 --> 00:03:32,956
<font color="#FFFFFF">The true dynamics are deliberately mixed,</font>

51
00:03:33,036 --> 00:03:37,298
<font color="#FFFFFF">sometimes uniform and sometimes highly skewed
toward a few states,</font>

52
00:03:37,378 --> 00:03:39,601
<font color="#FFFFFF">to create rich and varied behavior.</font>

53
00:03:39,681 --> 00:03:41,575
<font color="#FFFFFF">We study two coverage regimes,</font>

54
00:03:41,655 --> 00:03:46,839
<font color="#FFFFFF">a low data setting of fifteen episodes and a high
data setting of three hundred,</font>

55
00:03:46,919 --> 00:03:51,511
<font color="#FFFFFF">crossed with three levels of expert optimality that
create zero, three,</font>

56
00:03:51,591 --> 00:03:53,617
<font color="#FFFFFF">and six uncertain policy states.</font>

57
00:03:53,697 --> 00:03:59,210
<font color="#FFFFFF">Every reported number is averaged over one thousand
independently generated datasets.</font>

58
00:03:59,590 --> 00:04:03,600
<font color="#FFFFFF">The clipped posterior dominates both maximum
likelihood and the un clipped Bayesian posterior.</font>

59
00:04:03,680 --> 00:04:07,690
<font color="#FFFFFF">It guarantees one hundred percent accuracy on
states where the expert knows the single best action,</font>

60
00:04:07,770 --> 00:04:11,780
<font color="#FFFFFF">in every data and optimality setting,</font>

61
00:04:11,860 --> 00:04:15,870
<font color="#FFFFFF">whereas maximum likelihood ranges from sixty seven
to ninety two percent.</font>

62
00:04:15,950 --> 00:04:19,960
<font color="#FFFFFF">It also never selects an action outside the
expert's epsilon ball,</font>

63
00:04:20,040 --> 00:04:24,050
<font color="#FFFFFF">so it makes no truly bad mistakes.</font>

64
00:04:24,130 --> 00:04:28,140
<font color="#FFFFFF">Strikingly,</font>

65
00:04:28,220 --> 00:04:32,230
<font color="#FFFFFF">even though the constraints say nothing explicit
about which action to pick in uncertain states,</font>

66
00:04:32,310 --> 00:04:36,320
<font color="#FFFFFF">our method is still more accurate there than the
baselines,</font>

67
00:04:36,400 --> 00:04:40,410
<font color="#FFFFFF">which means the constraints implicitly transfer the
expert's uncertainty structure.</font>

68
00:04:40,790 --> 00:04:46,092
<font color="#FFFFFF">A central finding of the analysis is that more data
does not rescue maximum likelihood.</font>

69
00:04:46,172 --> 00:04:49,680
<font color="#FFFFFF">Even in the high data regime it keeps making bad mistakes,</font>

70
00:04:49,760 --> 00:04:52,278
<font color="#FFFFFF">choosing actions outside the epsilon ball,</font>

71
00:04:52,358 --> 00:04:57,660
<font color="#FFFFFF">because expert only data leaves large parts of the
state and action space unexplored no</font>

72
00:04:57,740 --> 00:04:59,887
<font color="#FFFFFF">matter how many episodes we collect.</font>

73
00:04:59,967 --> 00:05:05,454
<font color="#FFFFFF">Sweeping the optimality level from zero through
four confirms that our method holds across</font>

74
00:05:05,534 --> 00:05:07,248
<font color="#FFFFFF">degrees of expert optimality,</font>

75
00:05:07,328 --> 00:05:12,630
<font color="#FFFFFF">recovering policies that can even beat the expert
while keeping variance close to zero.</font>

76
00:05:13,010 --> 00:05:14,949
<font color="#FFFFFF">The numbers tell a clear story.</font>

77
00:05:15,029 --> 00:05:16,772
<font color="#FFFFFF">With a fully optimal expert,</font>

78
00:05:16,852 --> 00:05:21,331
<font color="#FFFFFF">our method reaches a Q star metric of exactly zero
with zero variance,</font>

79
00:05:21,411 --> 00:05:23,024
<font color="#FFFFFF">in both low and high data,</font>

80
00:05:23,104 --> 00:05:30,382
<font color="#FFFFFF">compared to fifty nine point seven five for maximum
likelihood and one hundred forty two for the plain posterior.</font>

81
00:05:30,462 --> 00:05:34,224
<font color="#FFFFFF">At the hardest setting, epsilon equals four with
high data,</font>

82
00:05:34,304 --> 00:05:36,308
<font color="#FFFFFF">we score eight point seven nine,</font>

83
00:05:36,388 --> 00:05:42,495
<font color="#FFFFFF">beating maximum likelihood's twenty point one one
and roughly matching the near optimal expert.</font>

84
00:05:42,575 --> 00:05:46,858
<font color="#FFFFFF">Accuracy on deterministic states is a constant one
hundred percent,</font>

85
00:05:46,938 --> 00:05:53,370
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the policy variance collapses from a standard
deviation near fifty two down to essentially zero.</font>

86
00:05:53,750 --> 00:06:00,581
<font color="#FFFFFF">By clipping a Bayesian posterior over the
transition dynamics with constraints derived from a near optimal expert,</font>

87
00:06:00,661 --> 00:06:04,521
<font color="#FFFFFF">you obtain gradient free offline policies that are
provably safe,</font>

88
00:06:04,601 --> 00:06:07,492
<font color="#FFFFFF">can outperform the expert who generated the data,</font>

89
00:06:07,572 --> 00:06:11,251
<font color="#FFFFFF">and carry dramatically lower variance than maximum
likelihood.</font>

90
00:06:11,331 --> 00:06:17,616
<font color="#FFFFFF">The same recipe of constraints plus uncertainty
also yields a ranking of actions in the uncertain states,</font>

91
00:06:17,696 --> 00:06:23,860
<font color="#FFFFFF">making the learned policies more informative for
high stakes planning such as clinical decision making.</font>
